  "submitted_text": "Step 1: Let the length of the third side be \\(x\\) units. According to the triangle inequality theorem, the sum of the lengths of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the length of the remaining side.\n\nStep 2: Apply the triangle inequality theorem to our triangle with sides 1, 3, and \\(x\\):\n- \\(1 + 3 > x\\), which simplifies to \\(4 > x\\) or \\(x < 4\\).\n- \\(1 + x > 3\\), which simplifies to \\(x > 2\\).\n- \\(3 + x > 1\\), which simplifies to \\(x > -2\\). This condition is always true since \\(x\\) is positive.\n\nStep 3: Combine the inequalities from Step 2 to find the range for \\(x\\): \\(2 < x < 4\\).\n\nStep 4: Since \\(x\\) is an integer, the only integer value that satisfies \\(2 < x < 4\\) is \\(x = 3\\).\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{3}",
